From 28e16b3c140bd8d284515cc99e28a4d564803d96 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Markus Schirp <mbj@schirp-dso.com>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 02:34:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add join_collapse_limit_excess to surface silent join-order
 degradation

When a query's explicit JOINs exceed join_collapse_limit, the planner
silently stops flattening them and skips exhaustive join-order search for
that subproblem.  This is invisible: a query that accumulates joins over
time (ORM eager-loads, generated SQL, nested views) can cross the limit
and get a substantially worse plan with no signal other than "it got
slow", and nothing points at join_collapse_limit as the cause.  This has
been the source of production incidents that were hard to diagnose.

Add a PGC_USERSET enum GUC, join_collapse_limit_excess, controlling what
happens at that point:

  off    historical behavior; stay silent (default)
  warn   emit a WARNING naming the FROM items involved
  error  raise an ERROR, preventing execution

warn suits observability (logging, CI); error suits fail-fast in
development and test.  The default is off, so installations that do not
opt in are unaffected.

deconstruct_recurse() reaches the collapse decision once per JoinExpr,
so a chain of explicit joins crosses the limit once per join.  Reporting
on the spot would emit one diagnostic per join rather than one per
chain, so mark the jointree item instead and have deconstruct_jointree()
report only those subproblems that no other flagged subproblem encloses.
A chain therefore yields a single report naming its outermost construct,
while parts of a statement independent of one another are each reported
in their own right.

The report names the FROM items of that subproblem and states how many
would have to be considered together, and the hint gives the value
join_collapse_limit needs in order to let the planner order them.  Both
modes emit identical text, differing only in severity.

Because the check runs during planning, it applies to any planned query,
including those planned via SPI (e.g. PL/pgSQL); the error is a normal,
trappable program_limit_exceeded exception.
